1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,960
Well, this is a week where we're all going to be looking for the right words to say,

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:08,280
And I don't always know what to say.

3
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:10,280
I know that we're part of a network.

4
00:00:10,280 --> 00:00:16,160
I mean, we join hands with parents and volunteers and teachers and all the people who care for

5
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:19,640
and protect and help our children,

6
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:26,160
And, after the horrible tragedy that I'm full of in Newton, Connecticut, we just really

7
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,560
can be at a loss for words.

8
00:00:28,560 --> 00:00:34,040
At one point, though, we're going to talk to our children and we're going to talk to

9
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:38,720
our scouts about these types of tragic events,

10
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:43,840
And it's one way we can help relieve some of the anxiety and the anger and the uncertainty

11
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:47,100
we all feel about these types of things,

12
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:53,440
We can bring some perspective to them and we can find constructive action in response

13
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:54,840
to tragedy.

14
00:00:54,840 --> 00:01:00,960
You probably already heard or read what Fred Rogers had to say about events like this.

15
00:01:00,960 --> 00:01:06,240
He said: when I was a boy and I would see scary things in the news, my mother would

16
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:09,680
say to me: look for the helpers.

17
00:01:09,680 --> 00:01:13,040
You'll always find people who are helping

18
00:01:13,040 --> 00:01:18,080
To this day, especially in times of disaster. I remember my mother's words,

19
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:21,960
And I'm always comforted by realizing that there are still so many helpers, so many

20
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:24,620
caring people in this world,

21
00:01:24,620 --> 00:01:29,420
And that's Fred Rogers. Mr Rogers,

22
00:01:29,420 --> 00:01:35,060
I can remember myself, in 1968, when I was in second grade,

23
00:01:35,060 --> 00:01:41,740
Robert Kennedy was shot and he lingered in the hospital for a time before he died,

24
00:01:41,740 --> 00:01:48,700
And the events of those days loomed pretty large in our eight-year-old minds.

25
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:51,060
Were we safe?

26
00:01:51,060 --> 00:01:53,140
Were our parents safe?

27
00:01:53,140 --> 00:01:56,740
Why did somebody shoot Mr Kennedy?

28
00:01:56,740 --> 00:02:04,460
I can remember how fearful and how uncertain we felt

29
00:02:04,460 --> 00:02:09,340
When some great tragedy unfolds. our scouts are going to know about it,

30
00:02:09,340 --> 00:02:13,900
They're going to discuss it and they're going to wonder what we think

31
00:02:13,900 --> 00:02:17,380
They're going to have those fears and uncertainties

32
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:19,660
And we can help them by doing three things.

33
00:02:19,660 --> 00:02:25,820
The first and most important thing is to talk about this.

34
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:32,859
Our scouts will see images and stories on TV and that may make them anxious and fearful.

35
00:02:32,859 --> 00:02:36,420
They need to talk about these events and we need to watch for their expressions of fear

36
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:37,420
or disbelief.

37
00:02:37,420 --> 00:02:42,500
We need to be prepared to help them work through this.

38
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:50,700
The best thing for us to do is to give them uncomplicated, honest explanations, reassurance.

39
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:55,340
We need to respect their emotions and beliefs, even if we don't necessarily agree with or

40
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:57,700
understand them.

41
00:02:57,700 --> 00:03:05,060
In our role as scouts, I think it's especially important that we be impartial on any debatable

42
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:10,500
questions that touch on these events, whether they're social questions or religious questions

43
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:14,340
or political questions.

44
00:03:14,340 --> 00:03:20,860
Our scouts are going to want to know or explain the reasons and the motivations involved

45
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:27,140
And you know, when it comes to that, our best answers are just going to be questions.

46
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:32,180
We need to ask them what they think happened and why they think it happened.

47
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:34,620
We don't need to necessarily supply answers,

48
00:03:34,620 --> 00:03:40,980
We just need to listen to their answers and help them think this through,

49
00:03:40,980 --> 00:03:47,100
And we can also talk about the ideals of the Scout, Oath and Law and how they apply to these

50
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:49,340
sorts of things.

51
00:03:49,340 --> 00:03:52,020
I think also we can help them gain some perspective.

52
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:54,820
You know, children see the world so much differently.

53
00:03:54,820 --> 00:04:00,580
Their sense of proportion and scale is really very small and they will fear. things that

54
00:04:00,580 --> 00:04:03,340
have happened to others will happen to them.

55
00:04:03,340 --> 00:04:08,660
They'll discuss them with each other and they'll form their own opinions, and a lot of times

56
00:04:08,660 --> 00:04:12,940
these opinions will feed their fears and misapprehensions.

57
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:16,700
In the face of great tragedy, we all tend to feel overwhelmed and anxious-

58
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:17,700
I do,

59
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:20,140
I know you probably do-

60
00:04:20,140 --> 00:04:26,860
And we feel a heightened sense of uncertainty, and seeing stories like this repeated over

61
00:04:26,860 --> 00:04:30,300
and over again can be very alarming for children

62
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:37,140
And it can seem as though the event is happening over and over again.

63
00:04:37,140 --> 00:04:45,220
We know that these feelings of uncertainty and these feelings of anxiety will subside

64
00:04:45,220 --> 00:04:52,620
over time and that we begin to feel better and less anxious when we talk to and listen

65
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:55,820
to each other.

66
00:04:55,820 --> 00:05:01,780
The third thing you can do to help resolve this and the hearts and minds of your

67
00:05:01,780 --> 00:05:07,060
scouts and yourself is to find some constructive action.

68
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:11,740
Share the understanding that being afraid and angry or feeling sad or uncertain about this

69
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:13,219
sort of thing is natural.

70
00:05:13,219 --> 00:05:15,020
We all feel that way.

71
00:05:15,020 --> 00:05:19,780
What's important is what we do with those feelings.

72
00:05:19,780 --> 00:05:20,940
We talk about them.

73
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:24,100
We find constructive things to do with them.

74
00:05:24,100 --> 00:05:29,340
Using a letter of support or making donations to help victims, joining others in a prayer or

75
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:32,660
a moment of silence for the people who were harmed

76
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:37,660
And other constructive action is a way to join the people that help.

77
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:42,260
Now listen to your scouts and discuss these things with them and give them useful ways

78
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:48,140
to express themselves that they can use then for the rest of their lives.

79
00:05:48,140 --> 00:05:54,340
That's how we do our part in forming the next generation of helpers.

80
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:59,940
If you'll go to the post that contains this podcast, you'll find a link to supporting

81
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:05,979
the helpers that will give you the addresses of the different first responder agencies

82
00:06:05,979 --> 00:06:09,140
that were called to Sandy Hook School.

83
00:06:09,140 --> 00:06:13,700
It's a helpless feeling for first responders when there's so much tragedy and so much

84
00:06:13,700 --> 00:06:18,099
death and so little they can do,

85
00:06:18,100 --> 00:06:23,420
But they were there and they did their jobs and they continue to do their jobs

86
00:06:23,420 --> 00:06:26,140
And they need to know how much we appreciate that.

87
00:06:26,140 --> 00:06:32,100
Finally, the work that you do as a scouter is so very, very important.

88
00:06:32,100 --> 00:06:36,900
It's part of this network of care and support for our children

89
00:06:36,900 --> 00:06:39,460
And we need to pay attention to them.

90
00:06:39,460 --> 00:06:46,020
You have a powerful potential for doing good, for helping a child who's maybe a little

91
00:06:46,020 --> 00:06:53,900
misunderstood, for helping a child with difficulties or disabilities and opening

92
00:06:53,900 --> 00:06:56,060
the world a little bit for them,

93
00:06:56,060 --> 00:07:02,500
And I don't think it's too much to say that these efforts, no matter how small

94
00:07:02,500 --> 00:07:08,900
you may believe they are, can save so many children from the kind of despair

95
00:07:08,900 --> 00:07:15,780
and darkness that had to have been behind an event like this.

